// From the journal, not yet reached here (spec). How a program is read
// without running it, how it is made faster, and how its versions are kept:
//
// Levels of running. A class flagged as an initialiser is actually run, and
// everything inside it with it. A large function may be run only part way,
// with what it reached kept and shown. Everything else is interpreted
// abstractly: the method is called with the abstract class put on each
// variable, and only the initialising methods (`=`, `:`) are called for
// real.
//
// Anything that reads the outside answers with the abstract class, unless it
// is flagged `speculate`; `refuse` means it is not read at all, which is
// about the reading itself, not about what is done with what was read. The
// first pass interprets abstractly; after that the runtime is real and only
// what comes from outside is held back.
//
// `interpretable?` says a call may be run while reading. It has to be said
// explicitly, because a rewrite of `rm -rf SOME_DIR` must not be run to find
// out what it does; a change should un-flag it until it is flagged again.
// And it has to know when an answer may be kept: not when it is IO, not when
// it is not the same twice.
//
// Trust is by author. Code may be run as whoever wrote it, so `@player` is
// theirs, and what that author may do is answered once: network at all, only
// these domains, with or without data attached; write access only inside
// named directories, never the whole disk, and overriding that is a change
// the person makes by hand. "May receive but not send" is not a thing that
// can be promised — timings carry data — but "may not send this data or
// anything derived from it" is.
//
// Building is from a directory: everything inside it resolves, and a name
// used twice resolves to the nearest one in directory order, with a tool to
// say which when that is wrong. Forward references are resolved as they are
// found, so loading one file may file diagnostics for the others it touches;
// reloading a file reloads what it touches and reparses that.
//
// A file may be written in two languages — `.js.ray`, where the `.js` is
// interpreted and Ray's primitives are underneath it — and the editor says
// so with half of each icon.
//
// A refactor that keeps the meaning is a refactor; the language answers
// whether two programs are equivalent.
//
// Optimisation. Joint allocation. A flag that says an equivalence graph is
// wanted. The program says how it is built and what it is optimised for, per
// function. Optimisations are allocated while running, with preferences for
// what to do: rewrite what is stored, or change how it is read from now on?
// What does the one-time rewrite cost in time, energy and money, against the
// overhead of reading old and new differently?
//
// Optimising is paid for. The gap between the use that was projected and the
// use that happened is the saving; splitting it between whoever optimised
// and whoever benefits — half for a year, or without an end — is what makes
// it worth doing, and the same for contributions measured by the cost they
// save. That needs a baseline: how often it moves, how a change that makes
// something slower moves it back, how a saving is attributed to the change
// that caused it, and how a deliberately bad baseline is kept from paying.
//
// Storage is an optimisation like any other. A database is a backend for a
// frontend — how objects reference each other, how a filesystem reads — and
// nothing more, so: the frontend is not how it is stored; the optimisations
// are allocated, not chosen once; the usual databases only optimise one
// restricted shape (rows and columns) where arbitrary graphs are wanted; and
// a file's contents are a frontend too, so taking it apart into pieces that
// are stored and searched separately is another place to optimise.
//
// `Class$` is stored: in memory or on disk as needed, distributed when
// asked, an arbitrary graph rather than a grid. Reads are what cost, so they
// are what is minimised. Fields are versioned the way classes and functions
// are, so renaming one does not make a new object or rewrite every record —
// the whole version history has an identity. Uniqueness has to answer what
// happens when two separated stores both claim it: a conflict that forces
// one to change, which is a merge conflict. Partitioning by time or hash
// says which bucket a value falls in, and changing that means rewriting.
// When to patch the data and when to rewrite it is the same question again.
//
// Video and the other multimedia formats are the same: an optimisation of a
// simple interface — scenes in two or three dimensions, over time — for
// processor time and storage, with the same question of what has to be ready
// to read.
//
// Version control. Hybrid logical clocks and CRDTs underneath. A local
// version of the codebase, and your fork of it reachable by everyone else
// working in it, so there is always somewhere to push. A change applied to
// every stable version it applies to, respecting what those versions changed
// themselves. A change flagged as the one that actually works, and changes
// grouped so they land together. Testing your changes against the latest
// ones instead of merging the latest into yours. Bundling many changes into
// a new version. Version control kept in the file for editors that are not
// the Ether, by injecting labels before functions that a plugin hides.
//
// Versioning itself: a version number is arbitrary at too many levels, so
// there is one point of arbitrariness left — the major version, raised when
// the answer to "is there a breaking change in what others can see?" is yes.
// Major version 0 may break at will. Versions may themselves be versioned,
// for a patch that went out without the number changing.
//
// Compatibility forwards: a program ships with the implementation of the
// language spec it was written against, so an interpreter that does not know
// that version yet still runs it.
//
// How many references there are to a name is asserted like anything else, so
// the borrow disciplines are one dial: used exactly once, used at most once,
// unique within a scope until it is given back, or counted as none, one or
// many.
//
// Hot reloading, of abstract and of production code, with migrations at
// every level of the stack: what happens inside a loop that is running, what
// the editor shows, how it is tested. Which generalises to memoising every
// computed value against a hash of what it was computed from.
//
// Generating a migration from many versions to one, written above or below
// the function it migrates, and reading one method across all the versions
// it has.
//
// A way to ask whether something came from the standard library or from
// somewhere else.
//
// Packaging, as the `ether` tool spells it. `ether @c++ %` lists versions
// and `ether @c++ @` lists locations, which are every path from the name to
// a backend: `@clang/@linux/@apt`, `@clang/@windows`, `@github/releases`
// with a version or a branch, `@x86-64`, `@local` for what is installed
// here. `.ray` is reserved for the mapping between two of them — `@ray`
// against `@c++` — so `ether @c++.ray @gcc` reads as one. `clone`,
// `install` and `use` fetch and check out, `use --global` for everywhere; a
// tool has an `entrypoint.cli.ray`. What is fetched lives under
// `.ether/external/@/@clang/$/@windows/@x86-64/%/0.16.0`, where the
// top-level name is the checked-out version and `$` holds the branches, so
// a change made and then switched away from waits under `$/@<user>%branch`.

// And reading software nobody wrote here (the language index that compares
// them lives in `Ether/library/Index.ray`):
//
// A universal language to compare every existing language in is the point of
// the index. The measures are grammar (how verbose), runtime complexity (at
// which layer of abstraction), time and space in sequence and in parallel,
// and how complicated a program's access to its own structure is — and then
// how languages are told apart by those measures, without redefining every
// semantics by hand, using the implementations that already exist.
//
// Out of an executable or a source tree: the things that are distinguishable
// as items, which need not follow the data the code declares; what each item
// can do, to itself and to others; and what an item is for — which
// interfaces it reaches (is it shown, is it heard; is it the contents that
// are shown, or that it exists at all), what its visibility is meant to be
// (is it sent over the network, is it encrypted — and that is respected: a
// licence check is read as the visibility of the items behind it, which
// stands while the mechanism enforcing it does), how it is updated, and what
// it is equivalent to elsewhere. A chat message is a chat message on every
// platform; what differs — the ordering, how threads are drawn — is what is
// ignored to see the equivalence, and an accessible item has open
// alternatives named beside it.
//
// Those readings are then reused: a feature extracted from a binary on an
// ongoing instruction to look for it, its theme taken as it is or as
// something equivalent with a way to change it, and version control
// generated for the parts of a program when all that is given is the current
// state and the previous one. Interfaces are themes, which is the same
// separability of layers as databases and languages have; a version change
// has to be diffable, and the interface may not break on one that is not
// resolved yet — it falls back to the theme that is.
//
// Reading a program in from its source, from the platform's own library, or
// by dropping a link on it. Recognising which language and which version
// something was built with, the way a media tool recognises a format.
// Suggesting where a thing is equivalent to another or could be made
// general. Optimising per function. Filtering a program to refuse the
// language features it may not use, self-modifying types among them.
// Completing in the shell once a name is marked as a tool.
//
// A name that is partially filled in and then renamed in a new version is
// told to whoever filled it. The language server protocol is spoken, so
// editors that are not the Ether still see what the language knows.
